Second, to achieve the goal of negotiation, only through communication and equality between the negotiating partners. When the United States tries to increase the pressure, leaving no room for China to start negotiations, can the two sides really not reach a solution?
In fact, US domestic trade law is based on Article 301, which allows the president to take various actions, including retaliation, to cancel any foreign government action, policy or practice that violates international trade agreements. The "America First" policy underscores the US government's intention to protect its own interests.
Donald Trump initially proposed a 10 percent tariff on $ 200 billion worth of Chinese goods and later proposed a series of increases. The move is an extension of a new business plan that was important in the trade war.
Economist Jagdish Bhagwati once claimed that the United States was using Article 301 of the Trade Act to unilaterally exploit its weaker trading partners. Exploitation of trade through the abuse of US power remains evident in the current US-China trade war, which is slowly establishing US protectionism.
The economist expressed his belief that equality is the core of a win-win policy for both sides. Balance results from interaction and coordination between conflicting parties. But achieving this equality takes time and is a process that requires both sides to return to the negotiating table now.
Therefore, as long as the United States continues its self-centered policy of not giving justice to China and trying to increase its influence by raising tariffs, it is not a good way to bring China to the negotiating table. As we all know, China already seems willing to enter the negotiating table, but only if the United States leaves space for China to breathe easily before reaching an end to the trade war.
