(Phnom Penh): Phnom Penh CamControl officials claim that today, vegetables and fruits sold in the markets in Phnom Penh do not have as many banned chemicals as before, after traders and farmers are more aware of the impact These banned chemicals after CamControl officers inspected and instructed them.
During a search and inspection operation at the Old Market today, Kong Vuthy said his team had selected vegetables, fruits and other foods for primary testing and sampled nearly 50 high-risk samples. Yellow noodles, meatballs, beef, squid, shrimp, dried fish, puffed fish, sausage, fried noodles, large noodles, small noodles, seafood and other cakes are sent immediately in a mobile test vehicle to find banned chemicals.
However, during the search operation, Mr. Cheal Phal's team found a total of 21 kilograms of banned chemicals (borax) in the dry field, and 10.5 kilograms of pesticide residues in the cabbage were confiscated. Destroyed in place.
However, Mr. Kong Vuthy continued to advise all vendors to sell products in accordance with the technical specifications, distinguishing between appliances that contain toxic substances and food, banning the display of products, beverages, sun exposure and placement. Sell out-of-date products as well.
He also called on traders, distributors, wholesalers, retailers, manufacturers, importers and distributors to have a clear source before selling and stop adding chemicals to food products that harm the health of consumers and consumers. .
At the same time, Mr. Kong Vuthy confirmed that these activities were carried out in accordance with the order of His Excellency the Head of Delegation in charge of the General Department of CamControl and the President of the Phnom Penh Dry Port Branch, who thought about the safety and health of the people, and this activity was carried out. Like the early rain.
