(China): Ahead of the first 100 days of the China International Import and Export Fair, enterprises gathered in Shanghai at the National Convention and Exhibition Center last week to review the program.
"It is unprecedented that a country should consider supporting foreign companies and boosting their presence in their own country," said David Miller, chief executive of Ariete. "This is a great opportunity for us to meet potential buyers as well as promote our reputation among the general consumer."
About 100 companies specializing in clothing, jewelry and consumer goods, kitchenware and electronics are showcasing their products and bargain prices. A quarter of those companies are among the top 500 companies. Other companies are industry leaders from around the world, such as the United States, Japan and Italy.
Many Chinese state-owned companies, large private companies and small businesses have come to negotiate prices, and some have already agreed on prices. The exhibition will not only help suppliers, but it will also help traders.
The exhibition will include trade and investment demonstrations by country and the Hong Kong International Business Forum. Suppliers and traders here are just a small part of the expected number of people attending this fall event.
One of the most popular topics discussed at the BRICS summit in South Africa is artificial intelligence, AI and smart cities. People in the eastern Chinese city of Hangzhou, one of China's smartest cities, are beginning to enjoy the convenience that comes with technological developments here.
Technological advances were first introduced in medical services, where people could sign up for a consultation with a doctor using a mobile phone and without having to stand in line with a large number of people. In addition, they can also pay for medications and check their medical reports at all digital installations installed in the hospital.
At one of the best and busiest hospitals, doctors are using the latest technology to facilitate doctors in the community. There are also remote consulting services, and some doctors use voice recognition technology to increase the effectiveness of treatment and prescribing.
Digital technology has helped cities set up China's first online court. People no longer need to appear in court to be heard. This reduces costs and time.
Hangzhou's transformation into a digital and smart city is a model for other cities in China and abroad to follow. Technology companies work closely with public services. Chinese technology giant Alibaba, for example, has developed an artificial intelligence system called City Brain to facilitate traffic flow in collaboration with local police.
"This system can reduce travel time in the city's business district by 15%," said Min Van Lee, chief scientist at Alibaba's Alicloud. We can also halve the travel time of an ambulance. "We can achieve this by using the city brain system, which functions to predict the future traffic situation throughout the city."
With the help of big data and cloud storage technology, the city's brain system will not only streamline traffic, but also inform authorities on how to manage energy, security and public safety more effectively in urban areas.
