Number of seats in the 25 polling stations:
History of Elections in Cambodia:
Democratic elections in Cambodia resumed in 1993, following the October 23, 1991 Paris Peace Talks.
The first elections in 1993 were organized by the United Nations. In the first mandate, there were 120 seats in the National Assembly. Four political parties won seats: FUNCINPEC won 58 seats, the CPP won 51 seats, and Son San's Buddhist Democratic Party won 10 seats. Ieng Molly's seat and party Molika won one seat.
For the second term in 1998, the number of seats in the National Assembly increased to 122, and seven political parties contested, but only three parties won seats, including the CPP with 64 seats, FUNCINPEC with 43 seats and the Sam Party. Rainsy won 15 seats.
For the third term in 2003, the number of National Assembly seats increased to 123. In the third mandate, there were seven political parties competing, but only three parties won seats: the CPP won 73 seats, the Sam Rainsy Party won 24 seats and FUNCINPEC won 26 seats.
In the fourth mandate in 2008, there were still 123 seats, with 11 political parties competing. However, the party won only five seats: the CPP won 90 seats, the Sam Rainsy Party won 26 seats, the Human Rights Party won three seats, the Norodom Ranariddh Party won two seats and FUNCINPEC won two seats.
For the fifth mandate in 2013, there were 123 seats in the National Assembly, with eight parties competing. However, only two political parties won seats: the CPP with 68 seats and the CNRP with 55 seats.
His Majesty King Norodom Sihamoni, King of Cambodia, Calls on All Cambodians to Vote
(Phnom Penh): His Majesty King Norodom Sihamoni, King of Cambodia, has issued a message calling on all Cambodians to go to the polls for the 6th National Assembly election on Sunday. This July 29, 2018. This is according to the message of the King on May 18, 2018.
In the royal message, the King said, "The people are deeply loved! The 6th National Assembly election will be held on July 29, 2018. On this occasion, I would like to solemnly call on all eligible voters to go to the polls and elect a national leader for the development and prosperity of all sectors.
He added that the election on Sunday, July 29, 2018 is a general election that is free, fair, equitable and secret in accordance with the principles of multi-party democracy. Therefore, do not worry about oppression, threats or intimidation from anyone or any political party.
He urged all compatriots to exercise their right to vote according to their own conscience, who has confidence in any candidate, any political party freely.
NEC Tells International Observers That Elections Will Be Legal Without CNRP
(Phnom Penh): NEC spokesman Dim Sovannarom told international observers who came to observe the election in Cambodia that despite the absence of the CNRP in the election, the National Assembly election Sixth, it is still legal.
Mr. Dim Sovannarom made this statement at a briefing on the current situation in Cambodia on the afternoon of July 28, 2018 at the Sofitel Phnom Penh Phokitra Hotel, organized by the Civil Society Forum.
In front of nearly 300 international observers, Dim Sovannarom said that without the former CNRP, Cambodia's elections would still be free and fair.
Mr. Dim Sovannarom "I would like to answer some questions that Excellencies, Lok Chumteavs, Ladies and Gentlemen have heard, ask whether tomorrow's election is legitimate without the former CNRP?" Please inform Excellencies, Lok Chumteav that the NEC is a legal institution, a purely legal body, so answer that it is really right, really legal, there is no problem at all.
"So without the former CNRP, tomorrow's election will still be a fair election, still a free and fair election, no one will say that tomorrow's election will not be fair," he said. "Do your job as a legal institution, a technical institution, respect the constitution, respect the law, if the constitution requires the NEC to do so, the NEC must do."
Please be informed that in this election, more than 500 international observers from 42 countries are expected to observe, while the NEC has officially recognized 322 people and is reviewing 216 proposals.
Separately, for national observers, the NEC also claimed that there was an increase over the previous mandate. There are 99,612 national observers from 112 institutions recognized by the NEC in this election. Eighteen party presidents will go to the polls tomorrow, while two party presidents are not on the voter list
