Comfrel steals 21 illegal observers in Kampong Cham
(Kampong Cham): Mr. Kouch Chamroeun, Governor of Kampong Cham Province, on the afternoon of July 28, 2018, confirmed to Fresh News that the Committee for Free and Fair Elections in Cambodia (Comfrel) continues to select observers by 21 illegal volunteers in Kampong Cham province.
Mr. Kouch Chamroeun continued that the 21 people were called to attend the training course on July 23, 2018 at UME University, Kampong Cham Province. Among those selected to observe were two students: Chak Hay and Mao Sonetta, a second-year student at the Buddhist University.
The reason why both students withdrew from the observer candidacy was because they realized that the organization's explanation process seemed illegal and inappropriate. After seeing the illegality of Comfrel, the two students came to the press conference to reveal the secret.
Speaking at a press conference in Ampil Leu village, Ampil commune, Kampong Siem district, Kampong Cham province this afternoon, Chak Hay and Mao Sonetta said that at first they did not know that Comfrel had not registered as a unit to observe the election. Appointed the 6th legislature in 2018. Both said they received a considerable amount of money from the NGO.
According to the two students, the first package received 130,000 riels to attend the training course, the second package received 175,000 riels after observation, and the third package received 275,000 riels to walk. Interview people related to the election.
Meanwhile, the provincial governor of Kampong Cham added that the two had not been arrested because they did not know that Comfrel's recruitment of observers was illegal and had withdrawn because the organization had not registered. Apply to be an election observation unit from the National Election Committee (NEC).
(Phnom Penh): Mr. Sik Bun Hok, Chairman of the National Election Committee (NEC), told observers from Europe that there are still some foreigners trying to interfere in the election in Cambodia. Mr. Sik Bunhok further confirmed to the observers that due to such attempts, the provisions of the Law on the Election of the National Assembly on the punishment of foreigners who interfered in the election process in Cambodia were imposed.
Mr. Sik Bun Hok's statement was made when he allowed international observers from European countries to meet and learn about the process of preparing for the 6th National Assembly election on the afternoon of July 28, 2018. This is at the Cambodiana Hotel in Phnom Penh. The top observers are MPs and senators from Britain, Italy, the Czech Republic, Bulgaria, Russia, Serbia, Armenia, Belarus, and Moldova.
Speaking to reporters more than an hour after the meeting, Dim Sovannarom, a member and spokesman for the NEC, told reporters that during the meeting, observers from Europe also questioned Sik Bun Hok. The punishment and fine for foreigners in Article 149 of the Law on the Election of Members of the National Assembly.
Mr. Sik Bunhok responded to the international observer's question that this limitation is due to the fact that to this day, there are still foreigners attempting and trying to interfere in Cambodia's elections.
Mr. Dim Sovannarom continued, "So this law is very important for fines from foreigners from 5 million to 10 million riels for foreigners who intend to obstruct the election."
According to Mr. Dim Sovannarom, during the meeting, Mr. Sik Bun Hok described the election process. In addition to showing the election process, Mr. Sik Bunhok also described the bitterness of Cambodia from the past to the destruction and only was able to hold elections in 1993 with the help of the United Nations.
The NEC Chairman confirmed to the observers that Cambodia was able to hold its own elections in 1998 and also outlined some of the NEC's efforts and principles to make the election process run smoothly. Better, more accurate and acceptable to the people, the voters and the political parties. In particular, ensuring that the voice of the people is guaranteed through their elections.
Please note that in addition to meeting with the President of the NEC, these observers also met with the leaders of Cambodia's top institutions such as Samdech Vibol Sena Pheakdey Say Chhum, President of the Senate and Samdech Techo Hun Sen, Prime Minister of Cambodia.
