BEIJING (Reuters) - At midnight on Saturday, the longest-running lunar eclipse in the 21st century appeared in the night skies of some countries in South Africa. Europe, Asia and Australia. This was quoted from the Chinese news agency Xinhua in the morning of Saturday, July 28, 2018.
The lunar eclipse began at midnight on Saturday, July 28, from 1:30 am and lasted for almost two hours, ie 1 hour and 43 minutes past midnight. In general, the moon was completely swallowed from the sky from 2:30 to 4:13 the same day (Phnom Penh time).
The above period is shorter than the complete disappearance of the lunar eclipse, which can continue to last only 4 minutes, with the longest lunar eclipse occurring for 1 hour and 47 minutes. The last time a total lunar eclipse occurred in such a long time was on July 16, 2000, with a total duration of 1 hour and 46 minutes.
