Foreigner Letter: Sam Rainsy's Call for Civil War
This is the reality that Cambodia has been facing since Sam Rainsy, the former leader of the Cambodian National Rescue Party, called on the armed forces to revolt against the country's legitimate institutions. It is not an unreasonable question in the context of political controversy. It is the question of recalling the indisputable chain of events that has brought the country closer to these tumultuous situations.
He also urged the Cambodian Armed Forces to rise up against the government, in addition to calling for racism and extremism similar to Pol Pot's policies. It is a real call for civil war. He is a threat to the peace of the country and with its neighbors.
Cambodia has been at peace for almost 20 years. The 1991 Paris Peace Accords and the United Nations failed to bring peace. It was Prime Minister Hun Sen's "win-win policy" that brought peace in late 1998. Due to the actions of the government in less than 20 years, the Kingdom of Cambodia has achieved a remarkable rate of development and a real recovery.
Basic human rights, such as the right to health, education, housing and employment, as enshrined in the Universal Declaration of Human Rights (Articles 22 to 26), are a priority for those responsible for rebuilding affected nations. Destroying human resources and going through Western sanctions for more than 12 years.
Cambodians have already learned the lessons of Western hypocrisy. We propose "values and principles" when it comes to Cambodia, but we forget all that when it is a resource-rich country that the West needs.
By: Dr. Raul McGenna
