Finally, the National Bank of Cambodia, set by the Royal Government of Cambodia, has set an interest rate cap of 18 percent per annum, or 1.5 percent per month. This means that all banks, microfinance institutions and rural credit institutions must respect the decision of the National Bank of Cambodia to provide loans with a maximum interest rate of 18% per annum or 1.5% per month if not complied with. No, they will face fines and penalties as well.
Simply put, setting the interest rate at 18 percent per annum or 1.5 percent per month makes it easier for people to borrow at a lower interest rate. The implementation rate of 18% interest per annum starts from January 2017 until now.
At the same time, Samdech Techo Hun Sen once considered that lowering the loan interest rate to 18% is a contribution to solving the difficulties of people who need credit to solve their livelihood, which in the past a large number of people have borrowed money from microfinance institutions. Finance at high interest rates up to 30%.
According to the report of the National Bank of Cambodia for the first half of 2018 and the second half of 2018, despite the Royal Government of Cambodia's interest rate ceiling of 18%, the sector is still growing well. Interest rates on both the riel and the dollar have also fallen.
The National Bank of Cambodia (NBC) has stated that the Cambodian microfinance sector continues to grow in both scope and scope of operations, which contributes more to the continued promotion of the financial environment, especially the use of formal financial services by people living in rural areas, as well as development. Rural economy as well.
The National Bank of Cambodia (NBC) has stated that "raising the loan interest rate ceiling to no more than 18% per annum for microfinance institutions and rural credit institutions, combined with the high level of competition in the microfinance sector, has led to lower interest rates in this sector." Notably, from 30.92 percent in the first half of 2017 to 17.85 percent for loans in riel and from 23.67 percent to 17.15 percent for loans in US dollars.
The average deposit interest rate of MFIs decreased from 6.4% in the first half of 2017 to 5.64% in the first half of 2018 in KHR and from 5.07%. To 4.85 percent against the US dollar. The decline was due to the 18% interest rate cap, which contributed to MFIs lowering deposit rates to offset declining interest rates on lending.
Currently, there are a total of 77 microfinance institutions (7 microfinance institutions that accept deposits from the public) and 302 rural credit institutions that are certified by the National Bank of Cambodia. The MFI operates across 25 capitals and provinces with a total of 1,375 offices and employs 32,171 staff.
In addition, to make it easier for people to choose the right microfinance institution with a legal license, the National Bank of Cambodia has always issued regulations and set up a hotline for people to ask questions about the sector. Banking and microfinance.
In particular, in the first six months of 2018, 50 rural credit institutions operating in Cambodia were closed by the National Bank of Cambodia because they did not comply with the regulations and laws of the National Bank of Cambodia.
