Stung Treng Governor: Nearly 5,000 people affected by floods from Laos have been evacuated safely as the Sekong River rises to 12.1 meters
(Stung Treng): Nearly 5,000 people, equivalent to more than 1,000 families in four communes in Siem Pang district, Stung Treng province, which was flooded due to flooding from Laos after the collapse of the Sepan Senam Noy dam, authorities Help evacuate their homes in a timely manner without causing any casualties or serious injuries. This was confirmed by Stung Treng Provincial Governor Mr. Mom Saroeun on the afternoon of July 26, 2018.
The Stung Treng provincial governor also confirmed that as of 3:30 pm today, the water level of the Sekong River had risen to 12.1 meters, exceeding the emergency level of 11.5 meters. Only.
Mr. Mom Saroeun stated, "More than 1,000 families in four of the five communes in Siem Pang district, equivalent to nearly 5,000 people, were evacuated by our authorities before the flood." "Arriving safely to a safe place does not endanger the lives of any citizens."
However, the governor has not been able to determine the number of houses flooded, the main priority is to focus on saving lives first.
The Ministry of Water Resources and Meteorology also warned of a rise in the Sekong River without any signs of receding, but only a slow rise. In particular, for the condition of the Sekong River, there is no threat to the Mekong River. This was confirmed by the Director of the Cabinet and the spokesman of the Ministry of Water Resources, Mr. Chan Yutha.
At this point, he explained that the maximum water level in the Sekong River is 1,000 to 1,200 cubic meters per second, while that of the Mekong River is from 38,000 to more than 40,000 cubic meters. One second, therefore, even if the Sekong River flows into the Mekong River, there is no threat. The spokesman added that the magnitude of the impact was limited to both sides of the Sekong River.
The Sepan Senamnoi Dam, which is being built by four companies (South Korea, Thailand, Thailand and Laos) with a capacity of 410 megawatts, is expected to be worth $ 1.2 billion. The 2019 process collapsed on Monday evening, leaving hundreds missing and thousands more homeless as nearly 30 bodies were reportedly found.
APSARA Authority decides to repair a road nearly 3 km long in 1 village for the people of Lolay village, Bakong commune
(Siem Reap): In accordance with the requests and actual needs of the people of Lolay village, Bakong commune, Prasat Bakong district, Siem Reap province, located in the Angkor resort, APSARA Authority has decided to repair a 2,800-meter-long road that serves the needs of use. Villagers' daily.
During the groundbreaking ceremony for the road on the afternoon of July 25, Mr. Luy Chhorn, Bakong Commune Chief, confirmed that this road is an ancient road that the people of Lolay village regularly use, but this road was damaged by Landslides in some places have resulted in small and large potholes and others in narrow places, making it very difficult for people to travel.
Mr. Thinny Vithou, Deputy Governor of Prasat Bakong District, said at the groundbreaking ceremony that on behalf of the district authority as well as the local authority, he was very happy and thanked the APSARA Authority for deciding to repair this road for the people in particular. Mr. Sok Sangvar, Deputy Director General of APSARA Authority, who facilitated and paid for the renovation work.
Mr. Sok Sangvar, who presided over the groundbreaking ceremony, said that this village road is on the back of an ancient baray, but it is a necessity of the people to travel daily, after the APSARA Authority received a request for repair. From the people decided to send professional officers to study the possibility of repair to a level that does not affect the archeology.
After the study of the official, it was found that the repair of this road was done only by filling the land in small and large pits, clearing the hills and clearing some of the branches that leaned on the road, not being able to clear large scale or expand. Beyond the current road. The development of infrastructure in the Angkor resort must be carefully considered to avoid development that could affect the archeology or historical sites of Angkor.
