After receiving 100 coffins sent from the United States, North Korea claims to hand over the remains of US soldiers on Friday!
SEOUL: North Korea will return the remains of US soldiers killed during the Korean War to the United States on Friday after receiving 100 coffins from Washington. This was released by Yonhap News Agency on Thursday afternoon, July 26, 2018.
The repatriation of the remains of US soldiers killed in the Korean War (1950-1953) was part of an agreement reached between US President Donald Trump and North Korean leader Kim Jong Un in Singapore last year. June 12 ago.
In June, the U.S. military claimed that it had sent 100 coffins to the demilitarized zone between the two Koreas, and according to Yonhap, North Korea had received them with the expectation that Pyongyang would hand over the remains. The U.S. Army on Friday, July 27, coincides with the 65th anniversary of the signing of the 1953 ceasefire.
South Korean news agency Yonhap has not confirmed the number of US military remains that North Korea will return. But U.S. officials say Washington expects to receive the remains of the first 55 U.S. troops and that the DNA will be analyzed before being sent to Hawaii.
