Cancer is the number one killer of cancer in China. Every year, about 3 million people die from the disease, and the number of patients who come for treatment increases to more than 4 million a year.
"Die to Live" tells the story of a man who smuggled cheap drugs from India to China to treat leukemia or leukemia caused by patients in China. There is not enough money to buy drugs that are legally sold in the market.
"Over the past five years, we have sent about 20.7 million US dollars in aid, which sounds like a lot, but the cost of national health insurance is much higher," he said. 10 times.
Jin has been trying to add more cancer drugs to the national health insurance list that can reimburse patients. Even though health insurance services in some parts of China now cover the cost of Gleevec, many patients still suffer.
China is an important market for cancer drugs. But now the Chinese market is heavily dependent on imported drugs, and it will take more than 10 years to develop these drugs and cost 2-3 billion US dollars.
Song Rui-lin, executive director of the Chinese Association for Pharmaceutical Development and Research, said: "Pharmaceutical companies need to reap the benefits of a reasonable profit, and we can only reduce the cost too much if the drug companies. "Unable to get a decent profit, they will withdraw from the market, so what can patients rely on?"
China's health insurance program covers many people, but most of them focus on basic health care. Generally, cancer treatment is not guaranteed.
The cost of medicines in China is already 30-40% of the total expenditure on national health, which is very high compared to other countries.
Professor Liu Guan, from the Chinese Center for Health Economics Research at Peking University, says China's health system needs to be reformed. Because doctors have less time and less health care, they have to use some of the money from the sale of expensive drugs to pay for low-cost medical services.
China's health insurance regulator recently held talks with local and foreign pharmaceutical companies on lowering cancer drugs.
Insurers are also discussing whether they should add more drugs to the compensation list.
At the same time, Prime Minister Li Keqiang also intervened, urging regulators to speed up the work of lowering the price of cancer drugs and reducing the burden on many families.
