International Analysis: China is trying to build good relations with the world as the United States builds a dividing wall from the world
The US-China trade dispute comes at a time when both sides are vying to expand their global influence. Based on the principles of free trade. In particular, the unilateral measures taken by some countries to counter another country are contrary to the principles of free trade.
The United States is a founding member of the WTO, while China only joined the organization in 2001 after 15 years of negotiations. At the time, China was an industrial-based economy and heavily dependent on trade. The country is not yet strong in both trade and high technology.
But things have changed dramatically, with China withdrawing from its WTO transition period and becoming a full member in 2011. China has shifted its economic chain to a more balanced economy in both trade in goods and services.
This shift was also reflected in the Doha trade negotiations between WTO member states, at which time China, along with other developing countries, became a major voice in legislative negotiations. Trade, however, eventually got stuck because of the inability to agree with the United States on many crises.
The stalemate has left many countries frustrated by the WTO Doha talks, turning their attention to regional trade agreements such as the Trans-Pacific Partnership (TPP). ) And the Regional Comprehensive Economic Partnership Agreement (RCEP).
In addition to tackling the country's economic inactivity, the One Belt One Road policy has also established ties between China and 76 other countries concerned, allowing Chinese norms and regulations to be heeded. And recognized online.
These trade trends have been strengthened recently. Beijing released its report on the WTO and set up two international trade courts in Shenzhen and Xian in June, which played a leading role in world trade.
The strategy proposed by China for the establishment of trade rules is to show support for developing countries, which is why China is gaining popularity. So while the United States is building a wall of protectionism, the Chinese government is focusing on building bridges of ties with countries around the world, through existing international organizations and through institutional or self-initiated initiatives.
