"Our target customers in Japan are Chinese tourists," said Zheng Hongmin, manager of WeChat Pay International Business at Tensen. We hope to provide our customers with an easy-to-use and detailed service that they used to buy for food, entertainment and accommodation during their travels abroad. ”
In Japan, mobile payment services have been slow to develop due to the increasing use of banknotes by Japanese people. Compared to China, Japan pays only one-fifth of the money without banknotes.
But today, more and more merchants are looking to partner with mobile payment companies to attract Chinese tourists. The Japanese government encourages further development of mobile payment services to boost tourist spending.
With the 2020 Tokyo Olympics approaching, Japan is looking for ways to develop a paperless payment system in its own country. This is an opportunity to expand the market for Chinese companies that provide electronic payment services. (China CCTV) Stung Treng Governor: Floods from Laos have not yet had a serious impact on some areas in Stung Treng.
This afternoon, FOX News reported that a dam in the southeastern province of Attapeu had collapsed, killing some people and leaving hundreds missing, and dumping 5,000 million cubic meters of water until authorities released them. Immediate operation to evacuate residents from the village.
However, the governor of Stung Treng province confirmed that according to the information he asked, there was no dam collapse, that is, the opening of the water by the Lao authorities in the Sepan area after heavy rain there.
Mr. Mom Saroeun confirmed that he inquired about this information to the Cambodian Consulate General in Laos and received the answer that during these days, heavy rain at the Sepan Dam forced the Lao authorities to open the drain as if there was no information. The dam.
Mr. Mom Saroeun added that for Stung Treng, Sekong and Mekong rivers, which receive water from Laos, the emergency level has not been raised and the fields and houses have not been flooded. However, his authorities and professional officers, as well as the three armed forces, are ready to intervene if this happens.
Ministry of Water Resources spokesman Chan Yutha also confirmed that the Sepan Dam has a capacity of more than 400 megawatts and a capacity of 5,000 million cubic meters. The Sepan River is a tributary of the Sekong River, and if it breaks, it flows into the Sekong River and then into the Mekong River.
Mr. Chan Yutha added that at this time, the Ministry of Water Resources and the National Mekong River Commission have been monitoring this issue and are ready to issue further notices to the public.
According to Mr. Chan Yutha, the distance from the Sepan Dam to Stung Treng, Cambodia is about 70 kilometers.
