(Pursat): Pursat is a province in the western part of Cambodia, nearly 200 kilometers from Phnom Penh. The province borders Kampong Chhnang, Kampong Speu, Koh Kong, Battambang and Thailand.
Due to the fact that Pursat is a province adjacent to the Tonle Sap Lake and the Cardamom Mountains, this province is highly favorable for natural tourism, both in terms of tourism on the surface of the Tonle Sap Lake and natural beauty along the ridge of waterfalls. .
Pursat provincial governor Mao Thunin said he was proud of his province's involvement in eco-tourism. He said that the tourist area in Pursat is divided into two areas: tourism on the Tonle Sap Lake and tourism in the mountains.
In addition to visiting the natural area of Pursat, which is the source of Pursat oranges, tourists can also visit Pursat orange orchards, as well as turmeric, pineapple, tamarind, and strawberry plantations.
In addition, a road connecting National Road 5 in Pursat to the border of Thmor Da in Veal Veng district, nearly 200 kilometers of natural forest and flexible hillsides is also becoming a tourist attraction. Tourists, too.
The governor of Pursat province said that he was considering with the relevant authorities clearing land at the foot of a mountain as a tourist destination so that he could stop at the foot of the mountain to see the natural beauty.
Due to the favorable nature of natural tourism, Pursat is attracting a lot of tourists. According to a report by the Pursat Provincial Department of Tourism, the number of tourists visiting Pursat in 2017 increased by more than 57 percent compared to 2016. According to the report, 2017 received more than 440,000 domestic tourists and nearly 8,000 international tourists, an increase of 15 percent compared to 2016.
