Earlier this month, China imposed an immediate 25 percent tariff on US agricultural products, such as soybeans, in response to the US imposing a $ 34 billion tariff on Chinese imports.
"In October, there will be two festivals that we will celebrate," said Zhang Yanlong, a soybean oil producer. One is the Moon Festival and the other is the National Day. This period is the most anticipated season for farmers in their harvest.
China is the largest importer of soybeans from the United States in the world, and most of the imported soybeans are genetically modified. Therefore, a 25% tariff increase on US soybeans will increase the cost of selling GMO soybeans in the domestic market. This is really good news for farmers who produce non-GMO soybeans as they can produce about 20% of the total soybean demand nationwide.
"We have not seen any immediate change in soybean prices," said Zhang Yanlong, a soybean oil producer. I think the change will be gradual. It can not recover immediately in a short time. "I am confident that our country will take steps to manage this change."
In 2017, China was the largest importer of soybeans, with imports in 2017 up 15% compared to 2016. Most of the imports are used in the production of animal feed and various vegetable oils. In recent years, many farmers have opted to grow corn instead of soybeans, which is less profitable.
