"The NEC considers that these activities are obstructing the election, which must receive a check from 5 million to 20 million riels under Article 142 of the Law on the National Assembly Election," he said.
However, Mr. Soryda has not yet revealed the names of former CNRP lawmakers who could face legal action.
It should be noted that on July 23, 2018, the Cambodian People's Party in Battambang province also filed a lawsuit against about 30 former CNRP officials for this clean finger campaign.
In the wake of this positive development, Rona Smith, the UN Special Rapporteur on Human Rights in Cambodia, issued a statement urging the Royal Government of Cambodia to clarify: "Obligations should be allowed." This statement is disturbing and unacceptable because it violates both national and international law.
Cambodia is a sovereign state and has sufficient rights to carry out its duties in accordance with its laws. Ms. Smith's demands are an interference in Cambodia's internal affairs.
According to the Constitution, a legitimate government is a government elected by the people through multi-party elections. If the government allows a call for a boycott, it is tantamount to contributing to the formation of an illegitimate government. Citizens will not obey the laws and regulations set by the government that do not arise from elections.
It should also be noted that the world condemned the Khmer Rouge for boycotting and called on the Cambodian people to boycott the 1993 UN-sponsored elections. At that time, Cambodian voters did not heed the call of the Khmer Rouge and voted for peace and development.
Now the UN Special Rapporteur, who has joined the opposition of the former CNRP, which is legally barred from political rights, is calling for a boycott of the election. But it is certain that eligible Cambodians will not heed this call, as they will go to the polls on July 29 to maintain peace and development. By: Chea Van Det, Member of Parliament
