Banking assets in Cambodia reach $ 31 billion, including 56.5% of loans and $ 19 billion in deposits
(Phnom Penh): Currently, the banking sector is growing rapidly, with the assets of banking institutions noted to increase to 31 billion US dollars.
This growth has made the banking institution expand its services more widely and conveniently by expanding its branch services to 862, including 1,593 ATMs.
Mr. Chea Chanto, Governor-General of the National Bank of Cambodia, said at the inauguration ceremony of Chief Commercial Bank on the evening of July 22, 2018, "This remarkable growth in the banking sector has increased the banking sector to 31B. "Million US dollars, of which loans accounted for 56.4%, while the capital of the institution was strong and raised deposits to 19 billion US dollars, contributing significantly to the expansion of financial intermediation."
He added that the significant growth of the banking sector in Cambodia has led to a steady influx of foreign investors, as is the case today. In Cambodia.
The recent arrival of Chiff Commercial Bank further reflects the growing trust and support of foreign investors in the banking and financial sector in Cambodia, which is one of the goals of the Banking and Financial Development Policy of the Royal Government under Led by Samdech Techo Hun Sen, Prime Minister of Cambodia.
Soeung Morarithy, CEO of Chief Commercial Bank, stated that Chief Commercial Bank of Cambodia obtained a license from the National Bank of Cambodia to conduct business as a specialized bank in Cambodia on December 5, 2013 due to the increase in banking services. Chiff Commerce is encouraged to expand its services.
He added that Chiffon Commercial Bank provides various services to customers such as: deposit accounting, individual loans, commercial loans and trade finance, etc., which are attractive features. In addition, Chief Commercial Bank will introduce credit cards and direct tax credit cards in the future.
At the Cambodia-China Agricultural Extension Center (CAPROC) in Phnom Penh, local farmers are taught various farming methods, from the use of machines to the care of seedlings and the cultivation of various vegetables. However, language barriers have hindered the effectiveness of the training.
"When we first started, we had translators to help," said Hang Wenkong, an agricultural machinery expert in China. "But the translators do not know much about the farming process and they do not know much about the technical terms we use."
To change this situation, Hang and his team of experts began learning the Khmer language on their own.
In addition, they also teach Chinese to Cambodian farmers because most of the tools and fertilizers they use are imported from China.
For a long time, Cambodia imported a lot of vegetables due to slow production in the country. But with the efforts of the Ministry of Agriculture and the support of agricultural experts, it is expected to increase production levels and reduce Cambodia's cost of importing more food from abroad. (China's CCTV)
