Procedure: A judgment that has no power to end a lawsuit, only a decision on a point that is a precondition for the final verdict. This type of verdict is intended to prepare and facilitate the adjudication of the case, and the court may issue this verdict only to the extent that it meets that purpose. According to Article 181 of the Code of Civil Procedure, there are four points that can be issued:
1: Judgment on the existence of the claim in case of dispute over the existence and amount of the claim. 2: Judgment that decides on the means of resistance or self-defense that can be judged independently.
Prepared by: Mr. Lim Sovannarong and Mr. Rath Chanthy, Member, Research and Compilation of Legal Articles of Cambodian Lawyers for International Cooperation
