The first official meeting between Donald Trump and Vladimir Putin was held in Helsinki, Finland on July 16 with the participation of 1,500 journalists from more than 60 countries. The one-on-one meeting was scheduled to last 90 minutes but was postponed for more than two hours.
In a joint press conference after the meeting, the two leaders stressed the importance of the summit. However, the meeting was seen as the beginning of a greater complication in the resumption of US-Russian relations, rather than the resumption of bilateral relations due to differences between the two countries. Both big brothers on some core issues.
Ahead of the summit, US allies in the West feared that Trump could reach a private agreement with Putin by turning a blind eye to Russia's interference in Ukraine in exchange for concessions. Russia to the United States on the Middle East.
As the two leaders try to share common interests as they continue to persevere in maintaining differences during the talks on Syria and Ukraine, Trump seems to be trying to demarcate the border with Putin to avoid Out of the accusation that he "conspired with Russia".
Recall that on July 13, the US Department of Justice announced the prosecution of Russian officials in connection with the interference in the 2016 US presidential election in order to put pressure on the dialogue between leaders. Both will force Tram to put more pressure on Russia.
But immediately after his return to Washington, Trump said he was confused, meaning he could not see why Russia was not interfering in US elections. That no. "I recognize the intelligence's conclusion that Russia interfered in the 2016 US presidential election," Trump said.
So far, there is no evidence that Trump won the election by cooperating with Russia. However, most Americans believe that Russia did interfere in the election.
His first statement that Russia was not involved seemed to mean that Tram was betraying his own nation. In addition to causing people to lose confidence in the world, it can also undermine the popularity of the Republican Party, which may be confused in the future.
