Mongolia: The number of people living with HIV has risen to 262 after seven new cases were reported in the second quarter of 2018, the Mongolian Bureau of Statistics said on Thursday. This was released by the Chinese news agency Xinhua on Thursday afternoon, July 19, 2018.
The office added that half of all people living with HIV were in their 20s and 44s, and that 99 percent were sexually transmitted. The first case of HIV infection in Mongolia was reported in 1992.
Since then, a total of 40 people have died and by the first half of this year, 21,827 cases had been registered in the East Asian country. But that number is down 16.3 percent from 2017. Kampong Thom Provincial Prosecutor Orders Police to Crack Down on Illegal Logging in Santuk District, Confiscate Nearly 50 Logs
