The United States reaffirms: There is no deadline for North Korea to denuclearize!
Washington: The United States has repeatedly stated that there is no timetable for North Korea's complete denuclearization. This was confirmed by the US State Department, quoted by the Chinese news agency Xinhua on Thursday afternoon, July 19, 2018.
The reaffirmation by State Department spokeswoman Heather Nauert is in line with what US President Donald Trump said a few days ago regarding the timing and pace of nuclear talks. Of Washington with Pyongyang.
It should be recalled that the nuclear disarmament agreement on the Korean Peninsula was reached at a historic summit between Trump and North Korean leader Kim Jong Un in mid-June in Singapore, and what is coming to fruition first. That is the North Korean agreement to hand over the remains of US troops soon.
Phnom Penh Governor Urges Professional Officers to Continue the Landfill Project to Deliver Land to Stung Meanchey Canals
(Phnom Penh): After the first step was completed and the land was handed over to the people, the Governor of Phnom Penh, Mr. Khuong Sreng, urged the professional officers of the Department of Public Works and Transport of Phnom Penh and the local authorities to continue the work. Fill the land to hand over the land to the people in Stung Meanchey canal, the second step and the next step as soon as possible according to the planned project.
In addition, Mr. Sam Piseth, Director of the Department of Public Works and Transport of Phnom Penh, also said that the construction site of the Stung Meanchey canal in the first phase of the construction work completed 226 meters on each side and a total length of 328 meters on each side. The team also installed 53 box drains. After completing the first step, the professional officer will continue to hit the second step, which is 252 meters long on each side.
It should be noted that in the Stung Meanchey canal area, the Phnom Penh Capital Administration has been constructing a 1,398-meter-long Stung Meanchey canal with 8 meters wide concrete, and the project is divided into four phases through two districts, Meanchey and Sen Sok. There are 573 families living and affected by the canal, and all of them will receive 4 by 6 meters of land legally owned on the spot.
