Digital payments are gaining popularity in China, with a growing community of users paying through apps such as WeChat Pay, Alipay and others in their daily lives. But many customers are also criticizing the denial of payment using banknotes in places such as tourist areas, restaurants and other shops.
This has affected the legal status of the Chinese yuan and consumers' right to choose their payment methods. This is according to the announcement of the People's Bank of China last week.
The statement pointed out that both banking and non-bank payment institutions are required to stop attracting businesses and individuals to discriminate against cash payments and to make adjustments to such practices over time. One month.
Digital payment may be required only if the store is not staffed and the customer agrees to pay this way only.
China's central bank has said that when promoting digital payments, businesses should not overreact to the concept of "banknotes". (China's CCTV)
