WASHINGTON (Reuters) - US President Donald Trump has said he acknowledges US intelligence allegations that Russia interfered in the 2016 US presidential election. But this statement is the complete opposite of what he said yesterday. This was released by the BBC on Wednesday morning, July 18, 2018.
Donald Trump on Tuesday spoke out in support of Russia, saying "there is no reason or evidence to accuse Moscow of interfering in US elections." Putin also denied that Russia had ever interfered in US internal affairs.
But on his way back to Washington, Trump immediately said he was confused, meaning he could not see the reason why Russia was not interfering in US elections. "I recognize the intelligence's conclusion that Russia interfered in the 2016 US presidential election," Trump said.
On Tuesday, US Democrats and Republicans strongly criticized the meeting between Trump and Putin, while US allies urged Trump to take a firm and clear stance on Russia.
