According to the polling station and counting ballots, there must be 3 guards, but can only carry one weapon
(Phnom Penh): Mr. Hal Sarath, Member of the National Election Committee (NEC), stated that at each polling station and counting ballots, there must be three security guards, but they can carry long weapons. Only one tree with a maximum of 30 bullets to protect security and order on the day of the upcoming parliamentary election.
Mr. Hal Sarath stated this at the meeting on the process of the 2018 6th National Assembly election campaign and security at polling stations and counting ballots, as well as demonstrating the quality of ink dipped in fingers. In the morning of July 17, 2018 in the meeting hall of the Ministry of Interior.
He said that on the day of the upcoming election, three security guards guarding each polling station and counting ballots must wear proper military uniforms and wear NEC security gloves until the end of their duties.
Electoral security agents have the duty to protect law and order from disturbance, harassment, intimidation and other threats or incidents that may occur and to train people who carry explosive weapons and other dangerous objects into the premises. Outside and inside the polling station and counting ballots.
In the case of multiple polling stations and counting of ballots in the same premises or building, the number of polling security agents for the polling station and the counting of ballots shall be appropriate to the actual situation, but shall not exceed three persons per polling station.
Mr. Hal Sarath added that election security agents must concentrate on guarding or preventing the transportation of election materials from one destination to another, as determined by the NEC and / or the Election Commission at all levels. .
The NEC member further claimed that the NEC has set up security and order for the National Assembly election in order to achieve some important goals, such as:
* Preventing and combating flashlight manipulation as well as attempts to sabotage or obstruct the election process in any form.
Ensure a calm, neutral political environment for free, fair and just elections in accordance with the principles of democracy, security, no threats, no intimidation and no violence.
* Provide security services to eligible voters, representatives or agents, candidates, political parties, candidates and national-international observers.
