The United States and North Korea have finally reached an agreement to restart the search for US military remains! (Video inside) (Washington): North Korea and the United States have reached an agreement on resuming the search for the remains of thousands of US soldiers who lost their lives in the Korean War (1950-1953). This is according to US Secretary of State Mike Pompeo quoted by Yonhap News Agency on Monday morning, July 16, 2018.
In a statement issued on Sunday (US time), Pompeo confirmed that the agreement was reached during a general meeting between the two countries, which agreed to begin discussions on arrangements. Repatriation of US Army remains. In addition, in June, the United States sent a coffin to the village of Panmunjom to receive the remains of its troops.
The last repatriation of US military remains by North Korea took place in 2007, and more than 440 US military remains were returned to the United States between 1990 and 2007. But the operation was suspended due to tensions between Pyongyang and the United States over North Korea's nuclear weapons and intercontinental ballistic missile programs.
