Two weeks ago, the United States imposed a 25 percent tariff on $ 34 billion worth of Chinese products. In response, China immediately imposed similar tariffs on US products.
According to the National Grain and Oil Information Center, a 25 percent tariff on US soybeans would increase the import price of soybeans by about $ 120 a tonne, more than $ 45 more than Brazilian soybeans.
"It will lead to a loss of US soybeans in the Chinese market," said Wang Lewai, a senior analyst at the National Grain and Oil Information Center of China. In fact, Chinese companies have been cutting soybean imports from the United States since June. Our data show that during the three weeks before June 28, China did not buy any new soybeans from the United States. "On the contrary, China has reduced its imports to 615,000 tons."
In the United States last year, about 36,240,000 hectares of soybeans were planted, the second largest in US history, with an expected yield of more than 121 million tons.
China is the largest US soybean export market. In 2017, China spent $ 14 billion to buy US soybeans, and nearly a third of all US-made soybeans were sold to China.
"In this situation, US soybean exports to China will decline," said Ching Guoqing, a professor at the University of Economics and Management in Tongzhou. "This is because they depend on yields from South America and soybean prices based on international markets."
Chinese experts say China can deal with the slowdown in soybean imports, and the Chinese Ministry of Agriculture and relevant departments are looking for solutions to reduce China's reliance on imported soybeans.
"We can reduce the demand for soybeans by adjusting the amount of soybeans for livestock," said Wang Lewai, a senior analyst at the National Grain and Oil Information Center of China. By adjusting supply and quantity, we can ensure a balanced market. "We believe that over the next year or two we will be able to fill the void left by the absence of American soybeans."
Experts say China has contributed 85 percent to global soybean trade growth for more than 20 years. And more countries will benefit from China's need for more soybeans.
"With the increase in US soybean insurance premiums and shipping costs after the import tariff, we will increase the import of soybeans from Asia and other countries," said Li Guoxiang, a researcher at the Chinese Academy of Social Sciences. Other countries. "And it will encourage these countries to increase their soybean cultivation." (China's CCTV)
