TOKYO: Prime Minister Shinzo Abe has vowed to increase aid funds as floods recede and weather begins to recover after a week of catastrophic floods in Japan's western prefectures. As of Friday evening, more than 200 people were still missing and dozens more were missing. This is according to the release by Xinhua on Friday evening, June 13, 2018.
Existing aid disbursed by the government is only about 2 billion yen (US $ 18 million), but the overall damage is projected by the Japanese government to be far greater. Additional funding needs to be approved to support the overall infrastructure rehabilitation, which can take up to a year.
At the 5th Japanese Cabinet meeting on Friday, the Japanese government led by Abe pledged to reach 35 billion yen (311 million US dollars) in emergency aid. To 58 areas affected by severe floods. The search for the missing continues.
