(Phnom Penh): The upcoming 6th National Assembly election on July 29 is considered as a free, multi-party democratic election in accordance with the principles of true democracy and in a warm and peaceful way. More.
In order to emphasize the democratic nature of the election, one should look at the following key elements:
First, there are 20 political parties competing for votes. The 20 parties are campaigning for their respective votes in an atmosphere of mutual respect. Each party is completely free during the registration phase, the campaign phase, in whatever form it can, respecting the rule of law. The number of parties has increased significantly compared to the 2013 5th legislative election, in which only eight parties participated.
Third, thousands of journalists from hundreds of units have registered to cover the content and images of all aspects of the election process. The journalists are from national and international institutions, including newspapers, radio, television and online media. The whole election scene will be monitored by the media to be widely disseminated both domestically and internationally.
Fourth, the election campaign was conducted in a good atmosphere without any significant violence or irregularities. Each party is completely free to campaign according to its purpose and means without any obstacles or obstacles. What is more noteworthy is that each party's election campaign is conducted with mutual respect.
Fifth, the people, investors, both domestic and foreign, as well as national and international tourists are also active without any worries about the election. Economic activities, tourism and trade are still alive and unchanged.
Only some foreigners and some Cambodians who want to destroy the election want to destroy Cambodia, who are slandering this election as undemocratic. Therefore, let the Cambodian people who love democracy with the rule of law and in peace, let's work together to protect this election from any destruction, because it is an election with the most complete democratic elements.
By: An Election Observer
