(Phnom Penh): Mr. Pan Sosak, Minister of Commerce, expressed his belief that the Cassava Policy Document will turn Cambodia into a reliable cassava processing and supply industry location for the world market as it provides a clear vision and approach. Establish key price chains to increase cassava yields and high prices to meet the needs of both domestic and foreign markets.
The Minister expressed his confidence on the occasion of presiding over the handing over ceremony of cassava policy documents held at the Ministry of Commerce this morning, July 10, 2018, with the participation of leaders and government officials. From the inter-ministerial representatives of the private sector and UNDP, which is a member of the National Cassava Policy Committee, about 50 people.
On that occasion, the Minister thanked the Department of Trade Policy for its efforts in collaboration with UNDP and relevant institutions to compile this important document to serve the development of Cambodia's cassava sector, the second most potential crop after the crop. Rice is a food product of the 21st century.
Dr. Richard Marshall, UNDP Economist in Cambodia, emphasized that the cassava policy will turn cassava into a high-yield, high-income crop for poor farmers and a product of industrial development in Cambodia.
He added that in order to optimize the implementation of policies, a governance structure has been established and the cassava price chain has been divided into sections to link with other priority sectors.
Please be informed that in 2017, Cambodia has become the eighth largest producer of cassava in the world. Since 2011, the area under cassava has been expanded from 400,000 hectares to 574,000 hectares in 2015, yielding a total of more than 13 million tons.
In the past, the Trade Integration Strategy (CTIS) has included cassava as a priority product to develop value chains and diversify exports.
Currently, the Royal Government of Cambodia, through the Ministry of Commerce, is working to boost cassava exports to China as the two countries have signed a protocol on preferential tariffs and phytosanitary and sanitary requirements and are studying Possibility of processing cassava and diversifying export markets.
