Public toilets in Beijing have been redesigned and easier to use. Beijing City Hall plans to replace 300 more public toilets this year for the city center. The new bathroom will be equipped with air conditioning and a deodorizing system.
Zhaoyang County Introduces First Bathroom with Ventilation System Currently, this district lacks an underground sewer system. The new system will help collect waste in the sewer by pumping it into a tank. The container will then be transported to a waste treatment plant.
"We want each bathroom to have its own style, budget and evaluation," said Zhou Xiaoxing, head of the environmental department of the Beijing Municipal Management Committee. By doing this, we can fully implement the bathroom operating mechanism and achieve the bathroom standard as well.
Rongcheng City in eastern China's Shandong Province has begun setting up sewerage networks to collect sewage waste to address rural poverty.
"The sewer is underground," said Jiang Thao, deputy director of the Rongcheng City Department of Urban Water Supply and Waste Management. It collects sewage from nearby villagers' houses. In total, the village has 27 sewers to collect waste from a few to more than ten houses, depending on the geographical location.
Since August 2015, Rongcheng City has replaced toilets in 744 villages, benefiting 116,000 families.
After the city hall changed bathrooms, residents in Suqian, near Jiangsu Province, also noticed improvements in the environment and hygiene in their bathrooms.
Currently, the city has 27 bathrooms that provide WiFi, cash withdrawal, phone charging and shoe polish. Cabinets, reading rooms and desks are also provided in these bathrooms. Toilet paper can be purchased using a machine that can scan people's faces.
Between 2015 and 2017, China spent more than 3 billion US dollars on the construction and renovation of 68,000 bathrooms in tourist areas. That is, exceeding the target of only 57,000 bathrooms.
Following this success, another 64,000 public toilets will be built or renovated by 2020 in order to boost tourism and make the sector more conducive to China's economic growth. (China's CCTV)
