Khmer king: Forests are a huge source of unimaginable economic value
(Kampot): His Majesty King Norodom Sihamoni, King of Cambodia, on July 9, 2018, said that the forest is a source of immense wealth with unimaginable high economic value. Estimated in terms of environment, culture, history, tourism.
His Majesty King Bhumibol Adulyadej made this remark on the occasion of His Majesty the King's presidency in the Royal "Tree Day" ceremony held in Prek Ampil village, Koh Touch commune, Teuk Chhou district, Kampot province on Monday morning, July 9, 2018.
The ceremony was also attended by Samdech Vibol Sena Pheakdey Say Chhum, President of the Senate, Samdech Ponhea Chakrei Heng Samrin, President of the National Assembly, Samdech Chao Feang Kong Sam Ol, Deputy Prime Minister, Minister of the Royal Palace, Samdech Krala Hom Sar Kheng, Deputy Prime Minister, Minister of Interior and members. Of the Senate, the National Assembly and government officials, about 5,000 people.
"Forests are an enormous source of unpredictable economic value in terms of environment, culture, history, tourism, education, protection of slopes, protection of biodiversity and habitat for stability," he said. Of human and animal life of all kinds.
His Majesty King Norodom Sihamoni also said that the celebration of the Tree Day reflects the efforts of the Cambodian people to unite to prevent the disaster of deforestation and to strengthen the spirit of ownership. Responsible for the future fate of their homeland.
He said the loss of forests, flooded forests, mangroves and vegetation could lead to catastrophic floods, hurricanes, droughts, ponds, lakes, streams, rivers and streams. For the needs of people, animals and plants, which leads to the balance of the natural environment, leading to the loss of all forests and fish resources.
The Tree Day was instituted during the Sangkum Reastr Niyum era under the glorious crusade led by His Majesty the King, His Majesty the King of Cambodia, His Majesty the King of Cambodia, the highest revered shrine since the 1950s, with the active participation of people from all walks of life. Became a widespread activity in the Tree Planting Labor Day movement in society, which was eventually recognized as a national tradition called the "Tree Planting Ceremony".
