(Phnom Penh): After the Royal Government of Cambodia succeeded in proposing to register the Sambor Prei Kuk temple site as a UNESCO World Heritage Site in 2017, an inter-ministerial working group has been conducting research at Koh Ker temple (Chok Korkor capital). And Banteay Chhmar to prepare for the registration of these two temples as World Heritage properties.
This was confirmed by Mr. Chuch Phoeun, Secretary of State of the Ministry of Culture and Fine Arts and Mr. Phan Nady, Director General of Sambo Prei Kuk National Authority in a press conference at the Ministry of Culture this week.
Mr. Phan Nady confirmed that the Royal Government of Cambodia as well as the Ministry of Culture and Fine Arts have been visiting the unique features of Koh Ker and Banteay Chhmar temples to meet the UNESCO requirements for the placement of these two ancient Khmer temples. A World Heritage Site.
Mr. Phan Nady said, "We are working to start and the first is the Koh Ker temple in Preah Vihear and the second temple in Banteay Chhmar, Banteay Meanchey, which we have already started."
Secretary of State Chuch Phoeun said that after the above two ancient temples, Cambodia will also identify the temples of Preah Khan, Kampong Svay in Preah Vihear province, the Angkor Borei temple in Takeo province and the old royal palace of the Udong period on Phnom Preah Reach Trep, Kampong Speu province. Prepare for the next UNESCO World Heritage List.
It should be recalled that UNESCO has decided to register three ancient Khmer temples and become a World Heritage Site, including: Angkor Wat in Siem Reap, registered in 1992, Preah Vihear in Preah Vihear in 2008 and Sambor Prei Kuk. In Kampong Thom province in 2017.
