Shanghai residents are happy with the news that tariffs on about 1,500 items have been reduced from 15.7 percent to 6.9 percent.
People in Shanghai are happy with the news, which makes them want to buy more.
"I'm going to buy some," said another Shanghai resident. "Reducing import duties allows me to buy good imported goods at affordable prices."
According to a survey by the Chinese Ministry of Commerce, 31 percent of Chinese people plan to spend more on imported products after the tariff reduction. Cosmetics, jewelry and baby products are all imported products. These tariff reductions are also expected to give retailers more confidence.
The tariff cuts are just one of a few tariff cuts the Chinese government has implemented over the past few years.
In December last year, the government reduced import duties on 187 items, including food, health supplements, medicines, clothing and entertainment products.
Experts agree that these efforts are aimed at boosting spending in China by giving them more options to shop.
This is the 13th area of China to be inscribed on the World Natural Heritage List.
The International Union for Conservation of Nature (IUCN), the official advisory body for the UNESCO World Heritage Site, said Fanjingshan has demonstrated its aesthetic value as a natural heritage site and has met the criteria of Conditions of World Natural Heritage. That condition requires that all areas included in the elimination round reflect the richness of biodiversity and integrity.
The ecosystem of Fanjingshan Mountain has preserved many ancient plants, which are rare, endangered and unique. It is home to 4395 species of plants and 2,767 species of animals.
Qin Changwai, Secretary-General of the National Commission of the People's Republic of China for UNESCO, said: "This new achievement is China's efforts to build an ecological civilization, and it shows the world the Chinese government's strong commitment. "And effective measures to build an ecological civilization."
